1
00:00:09,703 --> 00:00:14,008
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can you describe the Four Noble Truths?

2
00:00:25,652 --> 00:00:31,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, first and foremost, there are four descriptions or four aspects of one insight.

3
00:00:32,496 --> 00:00:34,297
[SPEAKER_01]: That insight being freedom from suffering, basically.

4
00:00:55,904 --> 00:01:11,882
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're free from suffering, that already implies the knowledge of what suffering is, that already implies the knowledge of what has been causing that suffering, that already implies the knowledge of freedom of suffering, and that already implies knowledge how to maintain that freedom from suffering, that insight.

5
00:01:16,207 --> 00:01:16,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Or how to develop it.

6
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:18,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, okay, yeah.

7
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:26,744
[SPEAKER_00]: How to maintain, which would basically then result in a complete liberation from all the upākalesa and all the other kind of pollutions.

8
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:36,349
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like one, two, three, four, that a person needs to discern one by one.

9
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:38,430
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically suffering and freedom from it.

10
00:01:41,972 --> 00:01:45,294
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, that's what, you know, in many suttas is mentioned basically.

11
00:01:45,814 --> 00:01:47,735
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be only, it would only be talked about

12
00:01:48,425 --> 00:01:55,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But the cause of suffering... and cause of suffering is, eh... nothing else, except craving.

13
00:01:56,931 --> 00:02:06,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, eh... again, that's something that... that people might read over, and then it would maybe just, like, seem like it's... oh, it's making sense, of course.

14
00:02:06,498 --> 00:02:13,823
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, in terms of the direct insight, seeing the craving is suffering, results in liberation from that suffering.

15
00:02:15,624 --> 00:02:17,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the craving means abandoning the craving.

16
00:02:18,372 --> 00:02:23,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only reason you crave is because you don't really see it directly, you don't understand it, you haven't discerned it.

17
00:02:26,254 --> 00:02:41,777
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... and, and, yeah, the important aspect is to recognize that whenever there is suffering present, you can then say, be, be, like, be, be, be, sort of, you can induce correctly that the craving must be present because the suffering is there.

18
00:02:43,777 --> 00:02:47,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not, eh, oh, I suffer, now I have to look for the reason.

19
00:02:48,427 --> 00:02:55,111
[SPEAKER_00]: You call it craving or something else, or also assuming that sometimes something else can be the cause of your suffering.

20
00:02:55,131 --> 00:03:10,379
[SPEAKER_00]: It really needs to be brought down so narrowly and so precisely, whereby you discern when the suffering, the disagreeable, unpleasant feeling that you're resisting, basically, when the suffering is present, means it's because simultaneously with it, there is craving present.

21
00:03:11,519 --> 00:03:15,261
[SPEAKER_00]: The absence of that craving simultaneously means the absence of that suffering.

22
00:03:15,281 --> 00:03:16,042
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no delay.

23
00:03:16,988 --> 00:03:22,530
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can have the direct knowledge of freedom from suffering directly verified for yourself.

24
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:27,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you cannot suffer amidst suffering, as the Buddha would often say.

25
00:03:28,511 --> 00:03:40,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, amidst things that used to make you suffer, the mind cannot suffer now, because there is no more craving in regard to those things.

26
00:03:40,674 --> 00:03:42,315
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a bit hard to abandon craving.

27
00:03:43,864 --> 00:03:44,865
[SPEAKER_00]: How to abandon craving?

28
00:03:44,885 --> 00:04:02,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, sense restraint, virtue, develop to the extent necessary, whereby you start discerning then your mental intentions, whether they're rooted in wholesome, unwholesome, whether they're rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

29
00:04:02,536 --> 00:04:10,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you know that they are, you do not engage in them, you do not tolerate them, you do not entertain them, you do not proliferate, you do not invest in them.

30
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:23,948
[SPEAKER_00]: That would basically enable the mind to then not resist the unpleasant feeling, to not indulge the pleasant feeling, to not try to distract itself from a neutral feeling.

31
00:04:25,209 --> 00:04:36,997
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the mind stops doing that, with the previous knowledge that it has heard from outside through the suttas and right teaching, when the mind stops doing that, with the previous knowledge of what craving is,

32
00:04:37,507 --> 00:04:40,348
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be able to see, ah, this is the absence of craving.

33
00:04:40,929 --> 00:04:43,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will be able to see right there, ah, there's no suffering.

34
00:04:46,331 --> 00:04:48,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll be also able to understand what that suffering was.

35
00:04:48,933 --> 00:04:55,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the suffering of the past, before he understood the way out, he wouldn't be suffering on account of that either.

36
00:04:56,456 --> 00:04:59,198
[SPEAKER_00]: The same knowledge would be applying to the present, past and the future.

37
00:05:13,254 --> 00:05:17,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, how to endure those painful feelings?

38
00:05:18,017 --> 00:05:19,078
[SPEAKER_01]: You have them now.

39
00:05:19,238 --> 00:05:20,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Painful feelings, cravings.

40
00:05:20,499 --> 00:05:20,919
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you...?

41
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:26,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you endure them, basically, if instead of enduring, you want to give in to sensuality.

42
00:05:27,902 --> 00:05:30,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you want to give in to a will or distraction.

43
00:05:30,204 --> 00:05:31,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you don't do that.

44
00:05:31,466 --> 00:05:33,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that would result in you having to endure them.

45
00:05:33,928 --> 00:05:43,418
[SPEAKER_00]: But sometimes, if you can sort of... you don't have to endure them, without having to give in to sensuality, aversion or delusion, then that's fine as well.

46
00:05:43,819 --> 00:05:47,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have to just constantly going looking for pain to endure.

47
00:05:47,663 --> 00:05:49,324
[SPEAKER_00]: They will not necessarily result in wisdom.

48
00:06:01,803 --> 00:06:05,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, how does one know that it's... you're freer from craving?

49
00:06:05,427 --> 00:06:07,109
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you know that you... Because there's no suffering.

50
00:06:07,289 --> 00:06:07,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's suffering.

51
00:06:11,113 --> 00:06:20,883
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no suffering because you cannot suffer, not because you're hiding away from it, or because you're not... you're just avoiding things that would... that would use to... that would use... that used to cause you to suffer.

52
00:06:21,123 --> 00:06:21,243
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

53
00:06:21,523 --> 00:06:24,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like, avoiding things means you're afraid of something already.

54
00:06:25,158 --> 00:06:32,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, say, I don't know, a person doesn't leave their kuti because they don't want to talk to anyone because it might annoy them and then they'll suffer.

55
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:33,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're not free from suffering.

56
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,282
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just protecting their precarious environment of non-disturbance.

57
00:06:38,702 --> 00:06:44,043
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you must protect it means you're disturbed by that, which means they're not truly not suffering.

58
00:06:44,063 --> 00:06:46,804
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just hiding away from the greater extent of it.

59
00:06:48,055 --> 00:06:51,939
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to reckon... that's why, like, you know, eh... a consciousness monk, he doesn't neglect his duty.

60
00:06:51,979 --> 00:06:59,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, certain things that you must do, they are not necessarily connected just... just sitting in a solitude and practicing, you know.

61
00:06:59,547 --> 00:07:05,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Like here, we live by ourselves, we do things ourselves, so we... we, you know, we have to go and fix the water line.

62
00:07:06,334 --> 00:07:07,435
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to go and patch my tank.

63
00:07:07,455 --> 00:07:09,898
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I don't have to, but then I won't have any water.

64
00:07:12,608 --> 00:07:15,090
[SPEAKER_00]: But having to do that doesn't bother you either.

65
00:07:15,150 --> 00:07:23,516
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeking out it as a distraction, but you're not running away from it when it arises on its own as something that's necessary to be done to sustain the way you live it.

66
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:34,505
[SPEAKER_00]: So you need to have, again, that self-honesty, so you know you're not hiding away, you're not indulging, you're not covering anything up.

67
00:07:35,297 --> 00:07:48,207
[SPEAKER_00]: With that self-honesty, and all the restraint of the senses, seeing the danger in the slightest fault, moderation in eating, that would result just in obviously being mindful of your actions, you start discerning mental intentions.

68
00:07:48,267 --> 00:07:54,692
[SPEAKER_00]: By discerning them, basically, the mind is already developing a certain perspective, because there is no craving without intention.

69
00:07:56,033 --> 00:07:57,874
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is intention without craving.

70
00:07:58,374 --> 00:08:01,297
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Arahant can still intend, and decide, and choose.

71
00:08:01,837 --> 00:08:02,818
[SPEAKER_00]: But he has no craving.

72
00:08:04,228 --> 00:08:08,171
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to see that, you have to see the intentions to begin with.

73
00:08:08,551 --> 00:08:10,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people are not even aware of the mental intentions.

74
00:08:11,614 --> 00:08:14,096
[SPEAKER_00]: They think intention is just the most obvious choice.

75
00:08:14,716 --> 00:08:15,217
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not.

76
00:08:15,377 --> 00:08:18,740
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just little inclinations, thoughts in themselves, that are already intentional.

77
00:08:21,222 --> 00:08:22,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's subtler.

78
00:08:22,242 --> 00:08:31,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order to reach the subtle level, this whole sense restraint, restrained by the patimokkha, by the virtue, moderation, idiocy and mindfulness, it's not... it's absolutely necessary.

79
00:08:31,850 --> 00:08:32,631
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unnegotiable.

80
00:08:34,427 --> 00:08:38,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And with the right instruction and the right way of looking, that's where the practice is.

81
00:08:39,010 --> 00:08:44,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Without the right instruction, it is just restraint, which is good, but it doesn't lead you to any knowledge.

82
00:08:44,955 --> 00:08:47,477
[SPEAKER_00]: But with the right instruction and that, that's all you need.

83
00:08:49,078 --> 00:08:52,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes people might think, okay, the restraint is not the dhamma.

84
00:08:52,460 --> 00:08:52,961
[SPEAKER_00]: That's true.

85
00:08:53,721 --> 00:08:56,803
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they would think, okay, well, now I got the dhamma, so I don't have to restrain.

86
00:08:56,883 --> 00:08:57,044
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

87
00:08:57,224 --> 00:08:59,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, dhamma applies only where the restraint is.

88
00:09:02,147 --> 00:09:02,808
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can see,

89
00:09:03,567 --> 00:09:13,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, restraint is not the dhamma, as in the application of the dhamma is not the same as the the necessary basis for that application, but you wouldn't be able to apply it if there is no necessary basis.

90
00:09:17,815 --> 00:09:22,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the mind practices like that, then the

91
00:09:23,409 --> 00:09:27,212
[SPEAKER_00]: The inclination in form of craving is becoming more and more obvious.

92
00:09:27,313 --> 00:09:27,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

93
00:09:27,593 --> 00:09:34,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you start recognizing that there are certain ways when you intend and incline, it makes you suffer.

94
00:09:34,779 --> 00:09:38,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes you feel too much, so to speak.

95
00:09:38,282 --> 00:09:39,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes you too involved.

96
00:09:41,405 --> 00:09:42,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Exposes you.

97
00:09:42,506 --> 00:09:43,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes you liable.

98
00:09:44,047 --> 00:09:46,670
[SPEAKER_00]: By discerning those things, you stop intending like that.

99
00:09:51,334 --> 00:10:04,701
[SPEAKER_00]: By not intending like that, you're not perpetuating the craving.

100
00:10:08,362 --> 00:10:09,523
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in terms of feelings.

101
00:10:10,383 --> 00:10:11,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

102
00:10:11,244 --> 00:10:11,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

103
00:10:11,624 --> 00:10:12,905
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always in relation to feelings.

104
00:10:12,985 --> 00:10:13,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

105
00:10:20,379 --> 00:10:43,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in an agreeable feeling that is present, you have to sort of discern the inclination towards wanting more of it, or towards wanting to stay longer, towards wanting to not go away so soon, towards wanting to increase it, to intensify it, to prolong it, any of those things.

106
00:10:46,219 --> 00:10:47,460
[SPEAKER_00]: In a disagreeable feeling,

107
00:10:48,267 --> 00:10:51,068
[SPEAKER_00]: It could be the general disagreeable mood, or something more specific.

108
00:10:51,809 --> 00:11:06,076
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to remove, or rather discern, and by discerning you remove it automatically, the inclination towards resisting it, towards trying to find a justification for it, towards trying to find an explanation for it, towards psychologizing it.

109
00:11:06,156 --> 00:11:13,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Just any of those things are mechanisms which you try to engage in, so to remove it, get rid of it.

110
00:11:14,925 --> 00:11:18,988
[SPEAKER_00]: And the subtlest of them is the neutral feeling, when it's neither pleasant or unpleasant.

111
00:11:19,088 --> 00:11:26,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when people give in to distraction, delusion, indolence, laziness, like mental heedlessness.

112
00:11:31,496 --> 00:11:35,779
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't do any of those three things, you're not perpetuating craving.

113
00:11:40,190 --> 00:11:46,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And at the same time, you've got to be able to... to feel, to be aware of your feelings.

114
00:11:47,036 --> 00:11:47,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, of course.

115
00:11:48,518 --> 00:11:50,560
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent that they have a reason on their own.

116
00:11:52,582 --> 00:11:55,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Because feelings arise having a reason, they cease on their own.

117
00:11:56,065 --> 00:11:57,006
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not saying that.

118
00:11:59,648 --> 00:12:02,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be... again, that's what mindfulness of feeling is.

119
00:12:02,310 --> 00:12:03,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning the nature, basically.

120
00:12:06,644 --> 00:12:13,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing that they have arisen on their own, this pleasant experience at the moment is arisen on its own, basically.

121
00:12:13,211 --> 00:12:13,631
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine.

122
00:12:13,671 --> 00:12:14,492
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't keep...

123
00:12:14,512 --> 00:12:16,334
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get rid of it, because pleasure means craving.

124
00:12:16,394 --> 00:12:20,318
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it doesn't.

125
00:12:20,358 --> 00:12:23,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaning too much towards that pleasure means craving.

126
00:12:23,561 --> 00:12:28,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaning away from the disagreeable means craving.

127
00:12:28,546 --> 00:12:30,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaning towards forgetfulness and distraction and

128
00:12:33,721 --> 00:12:38,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaning towards forgetfulness and distraction, that means craving in the neutral feeling.

129
00:12:46,033 --> 00:12:52,259
[SPEAKER_01]: So, really being aware of those leanings towards feelings... Yeah, that's a good way to start.

130
00:12:54,225 --> 00:12:58,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't like having... oh, this is my craving, because craving is in your attitude.

131
00:12:58,828 --> 00:13:07,513
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can... you can... you can bring it down so precisely, whereby you would see the... the... the... either the presence of suffering, or the presence of the possibility to suffer.

132
00:13:08,094 --> 00:13:11,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Any of those is generated by the amount of your leaning.

133
00:13:12,216 --> 00:13:16,979
[SPEAKER_00]: You're automatically generating possibility... liability to suffering, which is in itself dukkha.

134
00:13:20,001 --> 00:13:22,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you use that to know whether you lean or not.

135
00:13:24,819 --> 00:13:28,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognition of how liable to suffering does this make you.

136
00:13:48,192 --> 00:13:50,192
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no method that can show you that.

137
00:13:50,292 --> 00:13:50,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course not.

138
00:13:51,353 --> 00:13:51,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course not.

139
00:13:51,913 --> 00:14:00,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Because method is on the level almost like bodily action or certainly bodily perceptions where you're just sort of perceiving or going through some motions.

140
00:14:00,775 --> 00:14:03,355
[SPEAKER_00]: This is... we're talking about intentions behind your actions.

141
00:14:04,515 --> 00:14:08,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever method you do, you have to look at the intention behind, which means method becomes redundant.

142
00:14:08,416 --> 00:14:10,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means method is your sīla.

143
00:14:10,997 --> 00:14:12,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Method is your sensory strength.

144
00:14:12,617 --> 00:14:14,497
[SPEAKER_00]: That's as close as to method as you can get.

145
00:14:15,918 --> 00:14:17,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Method is your moderation in eating.

146
00:14:29,237 --> 00:14:29,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Shabu.

147
00:14:44,028 --> 00:14:50,273
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about, you know, noting every every movement of your mind, quickly write it down.

148
00:14:50,333 --> 00:14:52,675
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely misplaced, looking at the wrong place, yeah.

149
00:14:54,743 --> 00:14:56,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not seeing your intention to note.

150
00:14:57,288 --> 00:14:59,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you couldn't be noting your intention to note.

151
00:15:00,574 --> 00:15:05,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Noting is an action which is already intended, which is already leaned towards.

152
00:15:06,745 --> 00:15:07,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're not seeing that.

153
00:15:08,418 --> 00:15:13,300
[SPEAKER_00]: By not seeing that, no amount of engaging in that action will reveal its nature.

154
00:15:13,340 --> 00:15:17,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, the Buddha would have given a method and described it in the suttas.

155
00:15:17,661 --> 00:15:32,566
[SPEAKER_00]: When they did ask him what's the direct method, he said, a young man develops faith, leaves his home, lives restrained by the rules of patimokkha, practices sense restraint, sees the danger in the slightest fault, moderate in eating and devotes his time, basically, to wakefulness.

156
00:15:33,478 --> 00:15:44,549
[SPEAKER_00]: First watch of the night, second watch of the night, third watch of the night, and then he understands that the rebirth has been sort of destroyed, and I remember the first time I read that as a layman, I was also looking for a method.

157
00:15:45,109 --> 00:15:46,671
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought, well, this is just too general.

158
00:15:46,711 --> 00:15:50,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Why wouldn't he just give you a particular recipe how to become enlightened?

159
00:15:50,254 --> 00:15:51,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there isn't any.

160
00:15:52,096 --> 00:15:53,677
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the most particular you can get.

161
00:15:53,977 --> 00:15:57,920
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not seeing it in a direct manner, it's just because all the wrong views that are on top of it.

162
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:05,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to meet that Buddha's instruction halfway, basically, or even two-thirds of the way, to start to make sense.

163
00:16:06,305 --> 00:16:12,168
[SPEAKER_00]: But if person don't change any view, then they just add on top of their views, their notion of other practices, that's that's what you get.

164
00:16:12,709 --> 00:16:17,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Methods and modern vipassana techniques and whatnot.

165
00:16:17,311 --> 00:16:19,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Or methods of concentration and so on.

166
00:16:22,052 --> 00:16:27,155
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to look at the intention behind actions and you can't concentrate on that, because it's behind your concentration.

167
00:16:28,295 --> 00:16:29,796
[SPEAKER_00]: You can discern it, you can know it.

168
00:16:30,777 --> 00:16:36,800
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's said the consciousness is to be fully understood, not concentrated on.

169
00:16:42,581 --> 00:16:44,944
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't be aware of your intentions all the time?

170
00:16:45,304 --> 00:16:52,612
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you can be aware in that peripheral background manner, but awareness and concentration, in terms of techniques, is not the same thing.

171
00:16:53,433 --> 00:16:56,276
[SPEAKER_00]: People are not aware when they're doing meditation technique, quite the opposite.

172
00:16:56,796 --> 00:17:02,062
[SPEAKER_00]: They're practicing some sort of focusing exercise on one thing or the other, which means there's no general awareness there.

173
00:17:11,637 --> 00:17:13,338
[SPEAKER_01]: To have been able to see your intentions...

174
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:16,481
[SPEAKER_00]: They can only be seen indirectly.

175
00:17:20,164 --> 00:17:24,468
